<%# The keep control's per-visitor half — the one personal thing on an otherwise
    public page. Locals: painting, kept, count, autofocus

    It carries its own <turbo-frame> rather than only the contents, because a
    frame-targeted response has to hand back a matching frame id or Turbo reports
    missing content.

    THE BOUNDARY THIS PARTIAL SITS ON: the glyph is cacheable, the `button_to` is
    not. `daily/_day.html.erb` renders `_keep_glyph` directly as this frame's
    default content, so the mark is drawn in the publicly cached page and the
    rail never shows an empty box while the fetch is in flight. The button cannot
    go there — `button_to` emits a CSRF token, generating one starts a session,
    and a session puts a `Set-Cookie` on a page Thruster caches for everyone.
    `test/integration/favorites_test.rb` holds both halves of that.

    No visible words, so the `aria-label` is the whole announcement — see
    `decisions/0010-actions-become-a-rail.md`. `aria-pressed` is unchanged; it
    never depended on the label.

    `autofocus` is true ONLY on the write responses. Turbo replaces the whole
    frame on submit, which destroys the focused button and drops focus to <body>;
    autofocus puts it back, and focus landing on a toggle is also what makes a
    screen reader announce the new state. On the initial GET it is false — a
    fragment arriving unbidden must not steal focus, which is the same reason
    there is no aria-live here. %>
<%# No `class:` here, deliberately. Turbo replaces a frame by transplanting this
    element's CHILDREN into the one already on the page — the response frame's own
    attributes are discarded. The `.rail__slot` class lives on the frame in
    `daily/_day.html.erb`, which is the element that stays. Adding it here too
    looks load-bearing and is not. %>
<%= turbo_frame_tag "keep_#{painting.id}" do %>
  <%= button_to favorite_path(painting), method: (kept ? :delete : :post),
        form: { class: "rail__form" },
        class: "rail__act",
        autofocus: autofocus,
        aria: {
          pressed: kept,
          label: kept ? "Remove #{painting.title} from your collection"
                      : "Keep #{painting.title} in your collection"
        } do %><%= render "favorites/keep_glyph", filled: kept %><% end %>

  <%# The only word in the rail, and the reason it is a word. `3` alone would save
      nothing and throw away the one thing telling a first-time reader what the
      glyph beside it does — which matters more since the labels went (0010).

      Only once there is something to see. The compass's `Kept` covers discovery
      at zero; this one is the count climbing, which is the reward.

      `turbo_frame: "_top"` because a link inside a frame navigates the frame by
      default — without it, tapping this loads the whole collection page into a
      44px box and the reader goes nowhere. %>
  <% if count.positive? %>
    <%= link_to "#{count} kept", collection_path, class: "rail__count",
          data: { turbo_frame: "_top" } %>
  <% end %>
<% end %>
